- op: discuss  # 4951
  at: '2026-03-20T10:03:13Z'  # 4951
  by: agent  # 4951
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 4951
  clock: 4  # 4951
  nonce: '4951'  # 4951
  message: "Investigation complete: by design, not a bug. C/C++ analyzers only create call edges to functions with symbols in the project registry. Stdlib functions (fopen, fread, fwrite) have no local definition, so no edges exist for IO catalog to match against. Fix requires architectural decision: (1) create unresolved-external-call edges in C analyzer (inflates graph), or (2) have io-boundaries scan AST directly for IO function calls (scoped but significant). firejail gets IO matches because it has wrapper functions around stdlib calls that match the catalog."  # 4951
- op: discuss  # 15dc
  at: '2026-03-20T10:42:35Z'  # 15dc
  by: agent  # 15dc
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 15dc
  clock: 5  # 15dc
  nonce: 15dc  # 15dc
  message: "Same root cause confirmed for Java: 0/3 Java repos get IO boundary matches (commons-compress, jedis, gradle). Java IO is object-method based (socket.getInputStream().read()) but the Java analyzer only creates edges to project-defined classes (RedisOutputStream, RedisInputStream), not to java.io.* stdlib classes. The IO catalog has java.io.FileInputStream.read but there's no edge to it. This is the same 'only resolved symbols get edges' design as C. Affects C (3 repos), C++ (1 repo), Java (3 repos) = 7/42 repos across both curricula."  # 15dc
